### Facts
In Sitio Tagbacan, Barrio Casay, San Francisco, Quezon, Geminiano de Leon, together with his common-law wife Marianita Rosales and his father-in-law Juan Padernal, cultivated a parcel of land. A dispute arose between Geminiano and Juan over the division of the palay harvest. Geminiano insisted on a 50-50 share, but Juan asserted that the tenant share was only one-third.
On the morning of November 6, 1965, Geminiano and his brother, Constancio de Leon, were carrying sacks of palay from the field. Severo Padernal (Juan's son) and Pioquinto Ricohermoso (Juan's son-in-law) met them along the trail. Severo demanded that the palay be brought to his father's house, which Geminiano refused.

Suddenly, Severo unsheathed his bolo and attacked Geminiano, while Ricohermoso simultaneously unsheathed his bolo and hacked Constancio. When Constancio attempted to run to Geminiano’s aid, Ricohermoso pursued and hacked him again. Constancio fell, mortally wounded.

Meanwhile, Juan Padernal arrived at the scene. Instead of stopping the violence, Juan embraced and pinned Geminiano's neck and shoulders from behind. While Geminiano was held completely immobilized by Juan, Severo repeatedly stabbed and hacked Geminiano with his bolo until Geminiano collapsed. Severo and Juan then fled. Geminiano survived after intensive medical intervention, but Constancio died from multiple severe hack wounds.

Severo Padernal, Juan Padernal, and Pioquinto Ricohermoso were charged before the Court of First Instance (CFI) of Quezon with Murder (for the killing of Constancio) and Attempted/Frustrated Murder (for the assault on Geminiano).

Juan Padernal invoked the justifying circumstance of avoidance of a greater evil or injury under Article 11, paragraph 4 of the Revised Penal Code (RPC). He claimed he embraced Geminiano solely to prevent him from hacking or killing Severo, thereby seeking to avert violence between relatives.
The CFI convicted all three accused, finding conspiracy and treachery.

### Issue
Whether Juan Padernal was justified under Article 11, paragraph 4 of the Revised Penal Code (state of necessity / avoidance of a greater evil).

### Ruling 
1. No. The justifying circumstance of avoidance of a greater evil under Article 11(4) cannot be appreciated.
Under Article 11, paragraph 4 of the Revised Penal Code, three requisites must concur:
•	That the evil sought to be avoided actually exists;
•	That the injury feared be greater than that done to avoid it; and
•	That there be no other practical and less harmful means of preventing it.
The Court firmly rejected Juan's defense:
•	The "Evil" Did Not Exist on the Part of the Victim: Geminiano was not the aggressor; the aggression came directly from Severo and Ricohermoso. Geminiano was not hacking or threatening Severo when Juan grabbed him.
•	Injury Inflicted Was Far Greater: Juan’s act of immobilizing Geminiano did not prevent evil; it directly facilitated Severo’s lethal assault on the defenseless victim. Enabling the hacking and near-death of a victim is infinitely worse than the alleged confrontation sought to be pacified.
•	Presence of Less Harmful Means: If Juan truly intended to prevent a fight, he should have restrained his son Severo (who was the armed aggressor hacking at Geminiano) rather than pinioning the victim. Holding the victim while an armed relative hacks him is an act of complicity, not preservation.

### Ratio 
Dispositive:

Question:
Did the Supreme Court appreciate the defense of avoidance of a greater evil in favor of the accused in these cases? Explain.

